\name{BaseRInteractionLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRInteractionLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Interaction Plot Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{interaction.plot()} as the set of lines it draws: one series per
level of the trace factor, running across the levels of the x factor at
\code{fun(response)} for each cell.

\code{stats::interaction.plot} computes that grid itself and hands it straight
to \code{matplot}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{cells <- tapply(response, list(x.factor, trace.factor), fun)
matplot(xvals, cells, ..., type = type, ...)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

which is the shape \link{BaseRLineLayerProcessor} already reads for \code{matplot} --
one series per column, each point carrying its column name as \code{z}. So the
whole reading is recomputing \code{cells} and handing it over; nothing about
extracting a multi-series line is new here.

Recomputed rather than read back off the drawing, for the reason the
correlograms recompute theirs (#276): a cell mean is not on the page in any
form a grob carries, and the recorded arguments hold everything needed to
get it exactly as the function did.

\code{type} is not consulted. It varies the marks -- \code{"l"} draws lines, \code{"p"}
points, \code{"b"}/\code{"o"}/\code{"c"} both -- but every variant draws the same cell
means in the same series, and reading a \code{type = "p"} chart as loose points
would lose the trace grouping that makes it an interaction plot.
}
\keyword{internal}
